    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Hepatocellular Carcinoma (HCC) is the most prevalent form of liver cancer, the fifth most common cancer, and the third most common cause of cancer-related deaths worldwide. The vast majority of HCC patient mortality is due to the inability to surgically resect the tumor. c-Myc (MYC) is a proto-oncogene that is frequently overexpressed in HCC. Previously, the Felsher lab found that in transgenic mouse models, MYC-driven HCC (MYC HCC) is non-metastatic. Now we have found that overexpressing MYC and Twist1 leads to metastatic HCC (MYC/Twist1 HCC) in vivo and in vitro. However, the pathway by which these genetic events facilitate metastasis is not well characterized. Many studies show that a high density of macrophages in the tumor microenvironment correlates strongly with tumor aggressiveness and poor prognoses. Macrophages can be polarized to adopt the alternative growth promoting M2 phenotype, which may play a key role in altering the tumor niche to promote metastasis. To determine the molecular pathway for HCC metastasis, we investigated candidate genes that are differentially upregulated in MYC/Twist1 HCC compared to MYC HCC that also have regulatory functions in the immune system, specifically with macrophages. In our in vivo mouse model, we found that both MYC and Twist1 are necessary and sufficient for HCC metastasis. In our transgenic mouse models, we show that MYC/Twist1 HCC recruits macrophages to the primary tumor site compared to MYC HCC. In vivo, we demonstrate that macrophages are necessary for HCC metastasis to occur. Furthermore, MYC/Twist1 HCC cells polarize naïve macrophages to adopt the M2 pro-tumoral phenotype compared to MYC HCC. Our data provides insight into the mechanism by which HCC relies on molecular crosstalk with macrophages to induce metastasis, which can later be exploited for clinical advancements and create treatments to improve patient prognoses.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
